#!/usr/bin/python3
"""The FlickOS login screen's window (docs/design/flickos-greeter.md).

greetd runs /usr/libexec/flickos/greeter as the user _greetd, which starts the
cage kiosk compositor with this window. It lists the computer's users, asks
for a password and has greetd start the chosen session. It never sees PAM:
greetd does the checking (greetd-ipc(7) over $GREETD_SOCK).

What it shows:
- The users with a UID from UID_MIN to UID_MAX (/etc/login.defs) and a real
  shell, by full name, with accountsservice's picture or their initial;
  *Someone Else…* for a name typed by hand. The last user to log in is
  picked when the window opens, straight into the password box (ADR-005).
- The sessions: FlickOS, FlickOS (Plain Session), then the other Wayland
  sessions except bare labwc (ADR-006). Each user's last one is picked again.
- One card (clock, date, picture, name, password box) on one screen: the
  built-in one, found by its EDID size, else the first (ADR-003, ADR-007).
- Shut Down and Restart through logind, each with a second click, and the
  keyboard layout while the system has more than one (ADR-009). cage's
  XKB_DEFAULT_* say which: the layout can only be shown here, cage changes it.
- Its own zoom, Super+=, Super+-, Super+0 and Super+scroll (ADR-007).
- The background: the Dark wallpaper, or the picture Settings > Login Screen
  chose (Background=), and the card on the left, in the middle or on the
  right of its screen (Position=), from CONF_FILES (ADR-014, ADR-015).

What it remembers: STATE (owned by _greetd), the last user, each user's last
session and the zoom, written only after greetd accepted the session.

It exits 0 only after greetd accepted the session; any other ending (an
error, a bug) is non-zero, and the greeter script then runs gtkgreet instead
(ADR-010). Text people read is translated with gettext (domain
flickos-greeter); PAM's own messages through PAM's domain, Linux-PAM.

Usage:
  greeter-window           the login screen (needs $GREETD_SOCK and a display)
  greeter-window --check   print the users and sessions it would offer (the
                           boot test): "user<TAB>NAME<TAB>FULL NAME",
                           "session<TAB>ID<TAB>NAME<TAB>COMMAND",
                           "background<TAB>FILE", "position<TAB>PLACE" and,
                           with more than one layout,
                           "layout<TAB>SHORT<TAB>NAME" and "switch<TAB>KEY"
                           lines

Exit codes: 0 logged in (or --check), 1 error, 2 usage error.
"""

